//! The groups of a match: the bytes of it that each marked part of its
//! rule took.
//!
//! A rule may mark parts of itself as groups, with `Regex::group`. The
//! automaton does not know of them: it is the automaton of the same rules
//! without the marks, and a search finds the matches that it finds
//! without them. The groups are asked for afterwards, and found in the
//! bytes of that one match, which are known to match the rule from the
//! first to the last:
//!
//! ```text
//! rule      ([0-9]+) "-" ([0-9]+) "-" ([0-9]+)
//! text      on 2026-11-19 at noon
//! match        ----------                from 3 to 13
//! group 1      ----                      from 3 to 7
//! group 2           --                   from 8 to 10
//! group 3              --                from 11 to 13
//! ```
//!
//! Where the bytes of a match can be shared out in more than one way,
//! the rule is read from the outside in, and every part of it shares
//! out its own bytes among the parts inside it:
//!
//! ```text
//! one after another    an earlier part takes as many bytes as it can
//!                      with the parts after it still matching the rest
//! one | other          `one` when it matches the bytes, else `other`
//! a repetition         every time takes as many bytes as it can, and
//!                      one at least; its groups say the last time
//! ```
//!
//! These are the rules of POSIX for the groups of a match, as Sulzmann
//! and Lu's parsing with derivatives defines them, for the parts as
//! they are counted here, and they go with a search for the longest
//! match: what is written first takes the most. The parts that come one
//! after another are counted as they are written, however `then` nests
//! them. A group that the bytes did not come through is absent: one in
//! the branch that was not taken, or in a repetition of no times.
//!
//! ```text
//! rule      (a | ab) (c | bcd) (d*)
//! text      abcd
//! groups    ab, c and d            not a, bcd and no bytes
//! ```
//!
//! In one thing the parts are not those of POSIX: neighbours without a
//! group inside are one part, which takes the most that both can take
//! together. So the bytes of a group can depend on whether the parts
//! before it are groups, where in POSIX they do not:
//!
//! ```text
//! rule      (a | ab) (c | bcd) (d*)    with the last part as its
//!                                      only group
//! text      abcd
//! group     no bytes, at the end       the two parts before it are
//!                                      one, and take all four bytes;
//!                                      in POSIX the group is d
//! ```
//!
//! The groups are found by reading, not by trying. Every part that has
//! a group inside is asked one question of its bytes, and the answer is
//! one reading of them from left to right with the automata of what is
//! inside the part, groups left out; no byte is tried and taken back.
//! So what is inside a group, and beside it, may be any regex, with
//! complements and intersections, and the time and the memory of a
//! match are bounded when the searcher is built.
//!
//! For `first rest`, the automaton of `first` reads on, and at every
//! place where it accepts the automaton of `rest` begins anew. Of the
//! beginnings that come to one state the last is kept, and the last one
//! that accepts at the end is where `first` ends:
//!
//! ```text
//! parts     [0-9]+   then   "-" [0-9]+ "-" [0-9]+
//! bytes     2  0  2  6  -  1  1  -  1  9
//! first      ^  ^  ^  ^  x              accepts after every digit, and
//!                                       is dead after the dash
//! rest       x  x  x  ^-----            begins after every digit; only
//!                                       the beginning at 4 has a dash
//! ```
//!
//! With `first` dead and one beginning left, that one is the answer, and
//! the rest of the bytes is not read. `one | other` is the automaton of
//! `one` over the bytes. A repetition is the automaton of what is
//! repeated, begun anew at every place where a time can end; a
//! beginning that comes to a state where an earlier one stands is left
//! out, for the earlier one took more bytes in a time before.
//!
//! The groups of a match cost one reading of its bytes for every part
//! with a group inside that the bytes come through, times the
//! beginnings that are alive, which are no more than the states of an
//! automaton. The automata of all parts together have no more states
//! than the limit given to `Searcher::with_groups`; rules that need
//! more are refused. That limit bounds the time of a match, and it is
//! spent faster than the limit of the automaton of the rules: of n
//! parts in a row, each has an automaton and so have all the parts
//! after each, so the states grow with the square of n. Fifteen fields
//! with a mark between them make an automaton of 22 states, and their
//! groups need 366. Rules with a group under a complement or an
//! intersection are refused too: the bytes that match one do not come
//! through what is inside it.
//!
//! The groups of a match are written into a `Groups`, which the caller
//! hands in and gets back. One serves all the matches of a fold, and
//! nothing is allocated for a match:
//!
//! ```text
//! let groups = Groups::of(searcher);
//! let (sum, groups) = searcher.fold_groups(view, 0, groups, 0, step);
//! ```
//!
//! A `Groups` that was made for another searcher, or that another
//! searcher wrote into, may be handed in as well: one whose words are
//! not laid out as this searcher lays them out is left, and a new one
//! is made for the answer.
//!
//! - `program`: the questions of the rules, in the order they are asked,
//!   and the tables of their automata.
//! - `fill`: asking them of the bytes of a match.

import core::intrinsic::array;
import core::ptr::Generic;
import std::collections::pure::list::List;
import std::option::Option;
import std::regex::automaton::Automaton;
import std::regex::automaton::Found;
import std::regex::automaton::Match;
import std::regex::automaton::TooManyStates;
import std::regex::search::Occurrences;
import std::regex::search::Searcher;
import std::regex::search::Searching;
import std::regex::search::groups::fill::absent;
import std::regex::tree::Regex;
import std::result::Result;
import std::text::TextView;

mod fill;
mod program;

/// The questions that say the groups of a match, for every rule, and the
/// tables of the automata that answer them.
pub struct Program {
    // The questions, six words each: `program` has their parts.
    code: [u64; ?],
    // Four words for each rule: where its questions begin, where they
    // end, the number of its groups, the whole match counted, and the
    // number of its registers.
    rules: [u64; ?],
    // The rows of all the automata, one after another. A cell is the
    // place of a row in here, with the kind of its state in the three
    // lowest bits, as in an automaton. The first 256 cells are a dead
    // state.
    next: [u32; ?],
    // The column of every byte, 256 for each automaton.
    classes: [u8; ?],
    // What a `Groups` has room for, in this order: two places for each
    // group, the places between the parts, two lists of beginnings, two
    // words each, the step in which each state was last come to, the
    // number of those, and the number of the last step.
    slots: u64,
    registers: u64,
    marks: u64,
    threads: u64
}

/// The groups of a match: for each, where it begins and where it ends, or
/// that it is absent. Group 0 is the whole match, and the others are
/// numbered in the order they begin in, in the rule that matched.
///
/// It is made once for a searcher, with `Groups::of`, handed to every
/// question for the groups of a match, and given back with the answer
/// written into it. Nothing is allocated for a match, as long as no other
/// copy of the one that is handed in is kept, and it was made for the
/// searcher that is asked; for one made for another searcher, a new one
/// is made when that one will not do.
pub struct [value] Groups {
    // Two places for each group, and after them what finding them needs.
    slots: [u64; ?],
    // The rule of the match.
    rule: u32,
    // The number of groups of that rule, the whole match counted.
    count: u32
}

/// What a group took of a match.
pub enum [value] Taken {
    /// The index of the first byte of the group, and the index of the
    /// byte after it.
    Span(u64, u64),
    /// The bytes of the match did not come through the group, or the
    /// rule has no such group.
    Absent
}

/// Why rules with groups have no searcher.
pub enum [value] Refused {
    /// The automaton of the rules, or the automata that their groups are
    /// found with, need more states than the limit.
    TooManyStates(TooManyStates),
    /// The rule with this number has a group under a complement or an
    /// intersection.
    Misplaced(u32)
}

impl Groups {
    /// Room for the groups of a match of `searcher`, with no match yet:
    /// every group is absent.
    pub fn of(searcher: Searcher) -> Groups {
        Groups { slots: searcher.fresh(), rule: 0, count: 0 }
    }

    /// The number of groups of the rule that matched, the whole match
    /// counted; 0 before any match.
    pub fn len(self: Self) -> u64 {
        self.count as u64
    }

    /// The rule that matched.
    pub fn rule(self: Self) -> u32 {
        self.rule
    }

    /// The group `group` of the match.
    pub fn get(self: Self, group: u64) -> Taken {
        if group < (self.count as u64) {
            let start = array::get(self.slots, (2 * group) as i64);
            if start == absent() { Taken::Absent } else {
                Taken::Span { start, array::get(self.slots, (2 * group + 1) as i64) }
            }
        } else { Taken::Absent }
    }
}

impl Searcher {
    /// The searcher of `rules`, which may have groups: it finds the
    /// matches that the searcher of the rules without their groups finds,
    /// and says the groups of a match. The search for the states of the
    /// automaton of the rules gives up when it has found more than
    /// `limit` of them, and so does the search for the states of the
    /// automata that the groups are found with, all of them together.
    /// Those are more than the states of the automaton of the rules, and
    /// grow with the square of the number of parts in a row that have a
    /// group between them. The second automaton of a searcher that skips
    /// has the limit for itself, as in `Searcher::build`.
    pub fn with_groups(rules: List<Regex>, limit: u64) -> Result<Searcher, Refused> {
        match program::misplaced(rules, 0) {
            Some(rule) => Result::Err { Refused::Misplaced { rule } },
            None => match Automaton::build(rules.map(|rule: Regex| rule.to_bytes()), limit) {
                Ok(automaton) => match program::build(rules, limit) {
                    Ok(program) => Result::Ok { Searcher::skipping(automaton, limit).grouped(program) },
                    Err(error) => Result::Err { Refused::TooManyStates { error } }
                },
                Err(error) => Result::Err { Refused::TooManyStates { error } }
            }
        }
    }

    /// The groups of the match `found` of this searcher in `input`,
    /// written into `groups`: one that `Groups::of` made for this
    /// searcher is written in place, and so is any other that will do;
    /// else a new one is made.
    /// For a `found` that this searcher did not find in `input`, the
    /// groups say nothing that holds, and none when it is not inside the
    /// input. To take the groups of every match, fold with `fold_groups`:
    /// every call from another package counts a reference to the
    /// searcher and asks the text for its bytes.
    pub fn groups(self: Self, input: TextView, found: Match, groups: Groups) -> Groups {
        let filled = self.filled(input.unsafe_data_ptr(), input.len(), found, groups);
        // The length is asked for again so that the last use of `input`
        // comes after its bytes are read through the pointer.
        let count = if found.end <= input.len() {
            filled.count
        } else {
            0
        };
        Groups { slots: filled.slots, rule: filled.rule, count }
    }

    /// Folds over the matches of the rules in `input` from the byte `from`
    /// on, one after another, each with its groups. `step` is handed the
    /// groups of the match and hands them back, and the fold gives them
    /// back at the end, for the next text. `groups` is one of
    /// `Groups::of` for this searcher, or one that a fold or a stream of
    /// it gave back; any other is taken too, at the cost of a new one
    /// when it will not do.
    pub fn fold_groups<A>(self: Self, input: TextView, from: u64, groups: Groups, initial: A, step: (A, Groups) -> (A, Groups)) -> (A, Groups) {
        self.occurrences(input, from).fold_groups(groups, initial, step)
    }

    // The same searcher with the questions `program` for its groups.
    fn grouped(self: Self, program: Option<Program>) -> Searcher {
        Searcher {
            automaton: self.automaton,
            way: self.way,
            finder: self.finder,
            literal: self.literal,
            program,
            ends: self.ends,
            reach: self.reach,
            sets: self.sets,
            places: self.places,
            sought: self.sought
        }
    }

    // The words that the groups of a match need, with nothing in them.
    fn fresh(self: Self) -> [u64; ?] {
        match self.program {
            Some(program) => fill::fresh(program),
            // The whole match.
            None => array::splat<[u64; ?]>(2, 0)
        }
    }

    // The groups of the match `found` in the `length` bytes at `bytes`,
    // written into `groups`. A searcher without groups says the whole
    // match.
    fn filled(self: Self, bytes: ConstPtr<Generic>, length: u64, found: Match, groups: Groups) -> Groups {
        match self.program {
            Some(program) => fill::fill(program, bytes, length, found, groups),
            None => fill::whole(length, found, groups)
        }
    }
}

impl Searching {
    // The groups of the match `found` of the stream, written into
    // `groups`. The stream keeps `self` across the call, so the searcher
    // and its tables are read without counting references.
    fn grouped(self: Self, found: Match, groups: Groups) -> Groups {
        self.searcher.filled(self.bytes, self.input.len(), found, groups)
    }
}

impl Occurrences {
    /// The first match that begins where the stream stands or after, with
    /// its groups written into `groups`, and the stream after it. After
    /// `Nothing` the stream stays where it is and the groups are as they
    /// were handed in. `groups` is as for `Searcher::fold_groups`.
    pub fn next_groups(self: Self, groups: Groups) -> (Occurrences, Found, Groups) {
        // As in `next`, taking `searching` is the last use of `self`.
        let at = self.at;
        let barred = self.barred;
        let searching = self.searching;
        match searching.searcher.way {
            // As in `next`, the third word of such a stream is its credit.
            Skip => {
                let (last, start, end, credit) = searching.next_skipped(at, barred);
                if last == 0 { (Occurrences { searching, at, barred: credit }, Found::Nothing, groups) } else {
                    let found = Match { rule: searching.searcher.rule(last), start, end };
                    let groups = searching.grouped(found, groups);
                    (Occurrences { searching, at: end, barred: credit }, Found::Matched { found }, groups)
                }
            },
            _ => match searching.next_match(at, barred == at) {
                Matched(found) => {
                    let (at, barred) = Searching::after(found.start, found.end);
                    let groups = searching.grouped(found, groups);
                    (Occurrences { searching, at, barred }, Found::Matched { found }, groups)
                },
                Nothing => (Occurrences { searching, at, barred }, Found::Nothing, groups)
            }
        }
    }

    /// Folds over the matches left in the stream, one after another, each
    /// with its groups, up to the end of the text. `step` is handed the
    /// groups of the match and hands them back, and the fold gives them
    /// back at the end. `groups` is as for `Searcher::fold_groups`.
    pub fn fold_groups<A>(self: Self, groups: Groups, initial: A, step: (A, Groups) -> (A, Groups)) -> (A, Groups) {
        // `step` is passed along to the fold of the rest, as in `fold`.
        let (rest, found, groups) = self.next_groups(groups);
        match found {
            Matched(_) => {
                let (value, groups) = step(initial, groups);
                rest.fold_groups(groups, value, step)
            },
            Nothing => (initial, groups)
        }
    }
}
